@startuml state-appointment
!include _style.iuml
title Appointment lifecycle (enforced in domain/Lifecycle.scala)
hide empty description

[*] --> HELD : customer picks a time\n(POST /api/appointments/holds)
[*] --> CONFIRMED : customer books directly\n(POST /api/appointments)
HELD --> CONFIRMED : customer confirms before\nhold_expires_at (POST …/confirm)
HELD --> CANCELLED : customer releases the hold
HELD --> EXPIRED : hold ran out (system: booking flow,\njob move, confirm, or HoldSweeper)
CONFIRMED --> IN_PROGRESS : advisor, admin or the\nassigned technician starts\n(any time, also before the booked start)
IN_PROGRESS --> AWAITING_PAYMENT : advisor, admin or the\nassigned technician finishes\n(the customer has checked the vehicle)
AWAITING_PAYMENT --> COMPLETED : dealership admin\nrecords the payment
CONFIRMED --> CANCELLED : customer before the start time;\nadvisor / admin before work starts
CONFIRMED --> NO_SHOW : advisor / admin,\nafter the start time
CONFIRMED --> CONFIRMED : workshop controller / admin\nmoves the job (bay, technician)
COMPLETED --> [*]
CANCELLED --> [*]
NO_SHOW --> [*]
EXPIRED --> [*]

note right of HELD
  A hold lasts 10 minutes (HOLD_MINUTES).
  HELD, CONFIRMED and IN_PROGRESS take the bay,
  the technician and the vehicle: the database's
  EXCLUDE constraints apply to these three (V2).
  Cancelling or expiring a consulted appointment
  reopens its consultation request (BOOKED → APPROVED).
end note

note right of AWAITING_PAYMENT
  The work is done: the bay, technician and
  vehicle are free again (V3). The admin sees
  every job by status in "Jobs & payments".
end note
@enduml
